From fe6aeb77ce83e592b0c0cb341fe4a2de2282d725 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:47:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): speed up batch-1 flash attention decode
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Score the KV cells of the one-row flash attention path in blocks of
GGML_FA_KQ_BLK (32). Each block runs one vectorised softmax and rescales
the accumulator at most once, instead of a scalar expf and a possible
rescale per cell. A masked cell still skips its dot product and its V
update.

With an F16 K, Q stays in F32 and one Q load serves four K rows
(ggml_vec_dot_f16_f32_x4out). With an F16 V, the accumulator is F32 and
takes a mixed F16xF32 multiply-add. Both need AVX2+F16C+FMA or AVX-512
at build time. Other builds keep the old F16 accumulator and Q
conversion.

The per-thread scratch grows by GGML_FA_KQ_BLK floats. One helper,
ggml_fa_wdata_per_thread, now gives the stride to the writer, the
split-KV dispatcher and the partial reducer.

The output is numerically close to the old path, not bit-exact: V is
accumulated in F32, Q is not rounded to F16, and the softmax is
rescaled per block.

Ported from upstream llama.cpp PR #27478 (the ggml-cpu part), with one
fix. In the PR, ggml_vec_dot_f16_f32_x4out loads K with
GGML_F16_VEC_LOAD. With AVX512-FP16 (for example GGML_NATIVE on
Sapphire Rapids), that macro returns 32 unconverted halves (__m512h),
so every CPU source that includes vec.h fails to build. Here a local
GGML_FA_LOAD_F16 converts K to F32 explicitly. ggml_vec_dot_f16_f32
uses the same load and the same GGML_FA_HSUM, so the single dot and the
four-row dot stay bit identical and the AVX2 horizontal sum has one
copy. On AVX2 and on AVX-512 without FP16, the loads are the same as in
the PR. Two comments in vec.h and one in ops.cpp are reworded.

Checked with gcc -fsyntax-only for sapphirerapids, graniterapids,
znver4, skylake-avx512, haswell, ivybridge, x86-64-v2, x86-64 and
native. On Zen 2 the FLASH_ATTN_EXT dump of this commit is bit-exact
against the PR version in all 5317 cases.

